---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../../components/Footer.astro';
import KubernetesResourceCalculator from '../../../components/tools/KubernetesResourceCalculator.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Kubernetes Resource Calculator | InfraOpsX"
  description="Estimate Kubernetes workload requests, limits, schedulable node capacity, and Pod density for a homogeneous node pool."
  lang="en"
  canonicalPath="/tools/kubernetes-resource-calculator/"
  enPath="/tools/kubernetes-resource-calculator/"
  zhPath="/zh/tools/kubernetes-resource-calculator/"
>
  <Header locale="en" switchHref="/zh/tools/kubernetes-resource-calculator/" />

  <main class="tool-detail-shell">
    <div class="container tool-detail-container">
      <div class="tools-breadcrumb">
        <a href="/">Home</a>
        <span>/</span>
        <a href="/tools/">Tools</a>
        <span>/</span>
        <span>Kubernetes Resource Calculator</span>
      </div>

      <header class="tool-detail-header">
        <span class="section-label">KUBERNETES · RESOURCE PLANNING</span>
        <h1>Kubernetes Resource Calculator</h1>
        <p>Plan Kubernetes requests and node capacity for a homogeneous node pool.</p>
      </header>

      <section class="tool-section" aria-labelledby="calculator-heading">
        <div class="tool-section-heading">
          <span class="section-label">CALCULATOR</span>
          <h2 id="calculator-heading">Plan requests before deployment.</h2>
        </div>
        <KubernetesResourceCalculator locale="en" />
      </section>

      <section class="tool-info-grid">
        <article class="tool-info-card">
          <span class="section-label">HOW IT WORKS</span>
          <h2>Compare requests with schedulable capacity.</h2>
          <p>Kubernetes scheduling normally considers resource requests, not limits. This calculator normalizes CPU to mCPU and memory to bytes, applies the Planning Reserve to each node, and compares workload requests with the resulting schedulable capacity. The default value of 15% is an InfraOpsX Calculator capacity-planning default, not a Kubernetes or kubelet default systemReserved value. This estimate does not represent kubelet systemReserved, kubeReserved, or the actual Node Allocatable value. Limits are shown as planning and overcommit information, but they do not determine whether Pods fit.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="tool-info-card">
          <span class="section-label">IMPORTANT CONSIDERATIONS</span>
          <h2>This is a planning estimate.</h2>
          <p>Actual scheduling can also be affected by node allocatable values, kube-system workloads, DaemonSets, taints and tolerations, node affinity and anti-affinity, topology spread constraints, Pod max limits, hugepages, GPUs and other extended resources, ephemeral storage, uneven node sizes, and fragmentation of remaining resources. This result does not exactly reproduce kube-scheduler behavior.</p>
        </article>
      </section>

      <section class="tool-faq" aria-labelledby="faq-heading">
        <span class="section-label">FAQ</span>
        <h2 id="faq-heading">Common questions</h2>
        <details open>
          <summary>Why are limits not used to determine fit?</summary>
          <p>Kubernetes scheduling normally uses resource requests when deciding whether a Pod can fit on a node. Limits are displayed to show the workload's planning and overcommit profile, but they do not determine the fit status here.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What does Planning Reserve represent?</summary>
          <p>Planning Reserve provides headroom for the OS, kube-system workloads, the container runtime, DaemonSets, and operational overhead. The default value of 15% is an InfraOpsX Calculator capacity-planning default, not a Kubernetes or kubelet default systemReserved value. This estimate does not represent kubelet systemReserved, kubeReserved, or the actual Node Allocatable value.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does this exactly match kube-scheduler?</summary>
          <p>No. Node allocatable values, placement rules, topology, extended resources, storage, and the shape of remaining capacity can change actual scheduling decisions.</p>
        </details>
      </section>

      <section class="related-tools">
        <div>
          <span class="section-label">RELATED TOOLS</span>
          <h2>Pair workload planning with storage capacity planning.</h2>
        </div>
        <a class="button secondary" href="/tools/">View all tools</a>
      </section>

      <section class="tool-cta">
        <div class="cta-box">
          <div>
            <span class="section-label">INFRAOPSX</span>
            <h2>Need help with Kubernetes capacity planning?</h2>
            <p>Contact InfraOpsX for practical infrastructure guidance.</p>
          </div>
          <a class="button primary" href="/contact/">Contact InfraOpsX</a>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </main>

  <Footer locale="en" />
</BaseLayout>
